&lt;p&gt;The economy added substantially more jobs than expected last month, as more people entered the labor force and wages grew.&lt;/p&gt;
]]&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/11/business/economy/after-a-year-of-sluggish-hiring-2026-could-be-off-to-a-stronger-start.html</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Feb 2026 08:04:56 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Job Growth Was Overstated, New Data Shows</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/11/business/economy/january-jobs-report-revisions.html</link><description>&lt;![CDATA[
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/02/11/multimedia/jobs-revisions-wfql/jobs-revisions-wfql-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" alt="Article image"/&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
So-called benchmark revisions by the Bureau of Labor Statistics, an arm of the Department of Labor, have typically been small and attracted relatively little attention. But last year’s adjustment was the largest in years.
]]&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/11/business/economy/january-jobs-report-revisions.html</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Feb 2026 10:03:55 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Lower Unemployment Rate Supports Longer Pause for Fed</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/11/business/economy/fed-jobs-rates.html</link><description>&lt;![CDATA[
